To add metadata to path parameters and include extra information in the OpenAPI schema with FastAPI, you can use a few key features.

### Adding Metadata to Path Parameters

FastAPI allows you to add metadata to path parameters using the `Path` class. You can set various attributes such as `title`, `description`, and validations like `ge` (greater than or equal to) directly in the parameter declaration. Here’s how to do it:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Path

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
async def read_item(
    item_id: int = Path(title="The ID of the item to get", ge=0)
):
    return {"item_id": item_id}
```

In this example, the `item_id` parameter includes a title indicating what it is, and it is validated to ensure it is greater than or equal to 0. This information will appear in the automatically generated OpenAPI schema. [https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/path-params-numeric-validations/#declare-metadata]

### Including Extra Information in the OpenAPI Schema

You can use the `openapi_extra` parameter to add additional fields or configurations to the OpenAPI schema for a specific path operation. This is particularly useful for adding custom data. Here’s an example demonstrating this:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Request

app = FastAPI()

@app.post("/items/", openapi_extra={
    "requestBody": {
        "content": {
            "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "name": {"type": "string"},
                        "price": {"type": "number"},
                        "description": {"type": "string"},
                    },
                    "required": ["name", "price"],
                },
            },
        },
        "required": True,
    },
})
async def create_item(request: Request):
    return await request.json()
```

In this example, the `openapi_extra` argument allows you to define the expected schema for the request body, even if you opt not to use Pydantic models. This ensures your API documentation properly reflects the expected data structure. [https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/path-operation-advanced-configuration/#openapi-extra]

### Adding Metadata to Request Body Fields

For the request body, you can use the `Body` class to specify extra metadata and validations similar to path parameters. Here's an illustration:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Body

app = FastAPI()

class Item(BaseModel):
    name: str
    price: float

@app.post("/items/")
async def create_item(item: Item = Body(title="The item details")):
    return item
```

In this case, you can give the request body an overall title, which will be reflected in the OpenAPI documentation. [https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/body-multiple-params/#singular-values-in-body]

Combining these strategies lets you enhance the clarity and functionality of your API documentation generated through OpenAPI while providing a better user experience for both developers and clients.

